 ## Decisions
 
+### Why every row resolves a session's directory the same way
+
+A blocking-request badge asks which directory store owns a session, and a second resolver — the phone used to read the global session cache by id — answered that question with different fallbacks (no row session, no worktree path, no group directory). One session could then show its badge from its store on the desktop and from the additive index on the phone, which disagreed exactly after a lost settlement. `selectBlockingBadgeSessionScopes` is the only resolver: it walks the row's node, takes the session's own directory, then the node's worktree path, then the caller's group directory, and drops a session nothing can place. The row, the switcher and the phone all pass its scopes to `useSessionBlockingRequests`, so a session shows one badge everywhere; that lookup reads the cross-directory index only until the owning store's request snapshots are ready, and the store alone after, so no surface keeps a settled badge.
+
+### Why a collapsed header subscribes to a serialized directory signature
+
+`useCollapsedSessionActivityState` counts sessions by the store that owns them, and a session's directory can arrive after the header rendered, when the global session cache learns it. A selector that returned the resolved `[id, directory]` pairs as an array would hand React a new object on every global-sessions write and re-render every collapsed header. It returns one JSON string instead: the subscription follows the resolved directories, unchanged strings compare equal, and the scopes rebuild only when a directory actually changes. JSON keeps the encoding unambiguous for a directory that contains any separator.
+
+### Why a collapsed header draws marks without counts
+
+A collapsed group or folder header counts every session it hides, by the store that owns it, exactly as a row does: a group in a project that was never opened still shows a seeded request, and a form the index kept after a lost settlement stops showing once its store is authoritative. A session listed only as blocking, such as a hidden subagent, may have no row and no known directory, so no store can answer for it and the cross-directory index does (`selectIndexedBlockingRequests`). The header carries no counters: elapsed time is per session and means nothing for a group holding several running turns, and a request count would need the same per-session breakdown. Its marks sit inside the Show more button, so they are the row's bare glyphs, never a chip that reads as the control to press.
+
 ### Why merged cleanup asks the host whether a worktree is busy
 
 The per-directory status cache (`getSessionLiveActivity`) answers "unknown" for a directory whose child store was evicted, which happens after 20 minutes nobody looks at it. A worktree waiting for its PR to merge is exactly that directory, so a cleanup gated on "confirmed idle" from the cache waited forever. `readWorktreeUse` asks the host's active-session map, one request that covers every directory, and treats a failed request as unknown, so the next pass retries.
