diff --git a/packages/ui/src/sync/DOCUMENTATION.md b/packages/ui/src/sync/DOCUMENTATION.md
index feb30225a..2297c1100 100644
--- a/packages/ui/src/sync/DOCUMENTATION.md
+++ b/packages/ui/src/sync/DOCUMENTATION.md
@@ -1174,6 +1174,8 @@ Directories that are not bootstrapped get their initial activity from the host i
 
 Pending permissions and forms get the same treatment in `global-blocking-requests.ts`: the dispatcher feeds it `permission.asked`/`permission.replied`, `form.created`/`form.settled`, and `session.deleted` for every directory, and the host seed adds the `pending` map the server keeps from its own stream (`getPendingBlockingRequestsSnapshot` in `session-runtime.js`; dropped on reply, deletion, and OpenCode restart, so it carries no age cutoff). The index keeps only the fields its consumers render (`id`/`sessionID`/`action`/`resources`/`save` for a permission, `id`/`sessionID`/`title` for a form), which is also everything the host can carry. It is additive from the seed and a host snapshot never clears it by absence. A directory's own successful pending read does: `settleAbsentBlockingRequests` drops the requests the index held for that directory before the read and the answer lacks, per kind, for the sessions the read covered, and a later host snapshot cannot add them back, since the host may have missed the settlement or been read before it; a failed read drops nothing. A reconnect makes that read for every directory the index holds a request for, creating the store without a bootstrap when no row has. Directory stores stay the source for open directories; desktop and phone row badges also count the index's permissions and forms while that directory's store has not completed a bootstrap, because such a store learns only of live ones. Once the bootstrap completes, the store alone answers for the sessions it listed at that moment (`requestCoverage`), since its reply and reconnect paths clear requests the index can keep after a lost `permission.replied` or `form.settled`; a status change notifies form subscribers so rows switch source. A session that bootstrap did not list (created during a stream gap) reads the index too, also after it joins the list, because joining reads no requests; so a request the seed brings for it shows without opening its project. The index serves the tray's approval list and any surface without a mounted row. The mobile shell has no global poller, so its sessions sheet runs the seed after the list load it does each time it opens, and again when the stream reconnects while the list is shown (the iPad's sidebar, or a drawer left open in the background), since the events of the gap are lost; without it, phone rows miss every request already waiting at startup in a directory whose store has not loaded. A page an agent shows that waits for the user's answer (`render.page` with `awaitAnswer`) is a third kind, `pages` (`id` is the tool call id, plus `sessionID`/`title`). OpenCode never hears of that wait, so no directory store holds it: the server announces both ends as `openchamber:page-waiting` and `openchamber:page-settled` (`applyWaitingPageEvent`, placed by the session's directory when the server does not name one) and lists it in the seed's `pages`. Every surface counts it as a form: collapsed and expanded rows, mobile rows, run overviews and the tray, which only opens the session for either. Pages stay apart from `forms` so the form dock never tries to answer one. While one waits, the composer sends a message at once instead of queueing it, because the turn cannot end until the wait does; the message reaches the agent's plugin, which ends the wait.
 
+`selectIndexedBlockingRequests` reduces the index to whether a permission or a form waits for any of a set of sessions (`BlockingRequestKinds`); pages count as forms, since they block the agent the same way and have no store bucket of their own. It is the one scan of the index for a session no directory store can speak for (a hidden subagent listed only as blocking) and for a surface that never mounts a row. It returns one of four shared objects, so a selector built on it keeps its identity while the answer stands, and rows do not re-render on every index write.
+
 ### Why page snapshots replace waits while form and permission seeds stay additive
 
 The host owns waiting pages directly, so its complete `pending` map is authoritative for its own page waits. `readHostBlockingRequestsSnapshot` clears pages omitted from that map and overlays page events received during the read, including a settlement whose ask never reached this client. Otherwise a delayed snapshot can resurrect an answered page, and a lost settlement leaves a question badge indefinitely. Observers live only for the read and runtime reset invalidates them. Unchanged entries keep their references. Failed reads and hosts that omit `pending` clear nothing; isolated-space page waits belong to their own server and are left untouched. Forms and permissions remain additive because their host map only forwards OpenCode events and can itself miss a settlement.
